# +Point, -Surface
# +Left, +Right, -Length
#   * <module> The set-free series: the longest common subsequence, written by its definition
#
# A family of the set-free series, whose goal is that a typical problem of
# informatics contests, written by its definition, runs as fast as native
# code through a common standard library. A point is a closed pure lambda program, no set in
# it, whose answer is one unary numeral: the length of the longest common
# subsequence of two strings, Scott lists of Booleans.
#
# THE PROGRAM is the definition, recursion on the two lists:
#
#     lcs xs ys = xs 0 (\x. \xt. ys 0 (\y. \yt.
#                   equal x y (successor (lcs xt yt))
#                             (max (lcs xt ys) (lcs xs yt))))
#
# with equal the attractor merge_symbols_equal, successor and zero the unary
# constructors, and max a b = at_least a b a b, at_least the attractor
# natural_at_least of corpora/contest_attractors.pl. Written this way the
# recursion recomputes the same suffix pairs exponentially often; a setting
# that recognises the pair of suffixes by value gets the dynamic programme
# over the (n + 1)(m + 1) pairs, which is the textbook algorithm; native
# Prolog runs that programme on integers.
#
# THE POINTS: a DIRECT and a RESPELLED twin of each size, and a NEAR MISS, a
# proved trap for replacing the recursion by a native longest common
# subsequence: the same recursion without the successor on a match, whose
# answer is 0 for every pair of strings (by induction: every branch returns
# 0 or the max of two zeros), while the strings share a nonempty subsequence.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - dynamic programming over the pairs of prefixes: R. A. Wagner, M. J.
#   Fischer, The String-to-String Correction Problem, J. ACM 1974,
#   doi:10.1145/321796.321811, which solves the longest common subsequence
#   in time proportional to the product of the lengths;
# - the same in linear space: D. S. Hirschberg, A Linear Space Algorithm for
#   Computing Maximal Common Subsequences, Commun. ACM 1975,
#   doi:10.1145/360825.360861;
# - recognising the subproblems by value, tabled evaluation: W. Chen,
#   D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation with Delaying for General Logic
#   Programs, J. ACM 1996, doi:10.1145/227595.227597.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT is the unary numeral of longest_common_subsequence/3 for a
# point and zero for a near miss. PROOF. The program is pure, so S(P) = {N}
# for its normal form N. By induction on the sum of the
# lengths, lcs xs ys reduces to the unary numeral of the length of a longest
# common subsequence: the empty cases give 0; on a match the length is one
# more than for the tails (an optimal subsequence can be taken to use the
# matching heads); otherwise it is the larger of dropping either head.
# merge_symbols_equal on Booleans gives the Boolean of equality, and
# natural_at_least on canonical unary numerals the Boolean of a >= b (the
# prelude's definitions). The near miss reduces to 0 by the same induction,
# and 0 differs from the positive length of the strings chosen.
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   common_subsequence_point(?Point, ?Recursion, ?Spelling, ?Left, ?Right)
#
# The recursion is a constructor of set_free_dynamic/recursion,
# longest_common_subsequence or near; the spelling is a constructor of
# schema/spelling, direct or respelled.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
